{"response":{"docs":[{"iri":"http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CHEBI_754405","ontology_name":"chebi","related_synonyms":["Antipernicin","Behepan","Betalin 12","Calomist","Cyanocobalamin component of co 57 schilling test kit","Cyanocobalamin component of depinar","Cyanocobalamin component of dicopac kit","Cyanocobalamin component of rubratope-57 kit","Cyanocobalamin component of rubratope-60 kit","Cyanocobalamin component of vitaped","Cyanocobalaminum","Cyanovit-b12","NSC-80365","Rubivamine","Rubramin pc","Vi-twel","Vitamin b 12","Vitamin b(bdi1)(bdi2)","Vitamin b12 (as cyanocobalamin )","Vitamin b12 nos","cianocobalamina"],"ontology_prefix":"CHEBI","short_form":"CHEBI_754405","description":[],"label":"cyanocobalamin","obo_id":"CHEBI:754405","type":"class"},{"iri":"http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NCIT_C64333","ontology_name":"ncit","ontology_prefix":"NCIT","short_form":"NCIT_C64333","description":["A cobalt-containing coordination compound generated by intestinal microbes, and a natural water-soluble vitamin of the B-complex family that must combine with Intrinsic Factor for absorption by the intestine. Cyanocobalamin is necessary for hematopoiesis, neural metabolism, DNA and RNA production, and carbohydrate, fat, and protein metabolism. B12 improves iron functions in the metabolic cycle and assists folic acid in choline synthesis. B12 metabolism is interconnected with that of folic acid. Vitamin B12 deficiency causes pernicious anemia, megaloblastic anemia, and neurologic lesions."],"label":"Cyanocobalamin","obo_id":"NCIT:C64333","type":"class","exact_synonyms":["Berubigen","Betalin 12","CYANOCOBALAMIN","Calomist","Cobavite","Cyanocobalamin","Nascobal","Vitamin B12","cyanocobalamin"]},{"iri":"http://snomed.info/id/419382002","ontology_name":"snomed","ontology_prefix":"SNOMED","short_form":"SNOMED_419382002","description":[],"label":"Cyanocobalamin","obo_id":"","type":"class"},{"iri":"http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CHEBI_754405","ontology_name":"slm","related_synonyms":[